ZXPACK(1)                                    RETRO UNIX Z80

NAME
     zxpack - packed files in memory

SYNOPSIS
     zxpack
     zxpack file ...
     zxpack -d file ...

DESCRIPTION
     Files live in the kernel's elastic memory, the arena,
     either raw or packed. Packing is invisible: any read or
     write of a packed file unpacks it first, so cat, vi and
     exec never see the packed form.

     The kernel packs on its own:
     - when no process can run, it packs closed files that have
       not been touched for 15 seconds, giving way at once to a
       process that wakes up;
     - when memory runs out, it packs closed files and tries
       the allocation again.

     With no arguments zxpack shows the packed files, the
     bytes they save and the memory left. With files it packs
     them now; with -d it unpacks them.

     ls -l shows a z for a packed file and its full size; du
     counts the blocks it really uses.

THE CODEC
     LZ77 with a 2K window. Every decision - literal or match,
     each bit of a literal, of an offset and of a length - is
     coded with an adaptive binary range coder, so text and
     source code shrink to about a half and a third. Packing
     takes a few seconds and needs room for the packed
     stream; unpacking needs room for the whole file.

     This page is stored packed.

DIAGNOSTICS
     open, not packed        in use
     would not be smaller
     no room                 not enough free memory
